# GRADUATED in R44 from `known_gaps/` — this is now a LIVE regression fixture.
#
# It pins the `auto`-typed DICT and SET comprehension over a plain
# `Vector[int]`. Before the fix these produced SILENT WRONG VALUES —
# `auto dc = {x: x * 10 for x in xs}` gave `dc[1]` == 3 (expected 10) and
# `auto sc = {x for x in xs}` gave `sc.len()` == 0 (expected 3) — while
# checking clean and running without error, which is worse than the crash
# cells of the same family. Root cause (R44): the comprehension emitters
# minted their accumulator UP FRONT, before the loop body had produced an
# element, so the element type could only be a hardcoded guess
# (`Dict__int64_t__int64_t`) or the SOURCE collection's element type. The mint
# is now DEFERRED into a block left unterminated until the body has run, and
# takes its type from the MATERIALIZED element.
#
# RED-verified pre-fix on one build of each compiler: Rust `gg` printed 3 / 0,
# the self-host driver 0 / 8. Both now print 10 / 3, on C and on
# `--backend=llvm`.
#
# Wired test: `sound_auto_dict_set_comprehension_values` (tests/integration.rs).
void main():
    Vector[int] xs = [1, 2, 3]
    auto dc = {x: x * 10 for x in xs}
    auto sc = {x for x in xs}
    print(dc[1])
    print(sc.len())
